"""potential_field_v9_wasm — single-file agar.io submission (kit + stdlib +
whitelisted ``wasmtime`` only).

v9 (the shallow tactical search — see below) plus an **embedded WASM kernel**
that offloads the hot per-frame tactical evaluation. The kernel binary is
carried inline as a ``base85(zlib(...))`` literal, decoded and instantiated
ONCE before the pipe loop, and exposes ``score_candidates`` — one branch-free
pass that, for every candidate heading, moves our blobs a frame and scores them
against a split-aware effective-kill-radius opponent model (no ``sqrt``: squared
distances throughout). It drives a wasm-accelerated, split-aware tactical floor
(``_wasm_floor``) that supersedes v8's ``_safe_heading`` — v8's floor excluded
split reach, the exact gap behind the replay death; this one folds it in.

Robustness: the kernel is authored in WAT and assembled with wasi-sdk-free
tooling, and its Python fallback (``_kernel_eval_py``) is **byte-identical**
(same IEEE-f64 ops, same order — verified 0-ULP over random states). If
``wasmtime`` is absent or the grader sandbox blocks instantiation (gate G0 is
unverified), the loader silently disables and the Python model runs instead, so
behaviour is identical with or without working WASM — the kernel is pure
acceleration, never a correctness dependency. The full v9 pure-Python depth-N
search still runs underneath; this variant adds the accelerated floor on top.

NOTE ON DEPTH: this hand-written WAT kernel implements the depth-1 effective-
radius evaluation (its opponent model folds the enemy's move into a kill
radius, which is inherently single-frame). The multi-frame in-WASM search
belongs in the C core (``v2/wasm/search_core.c``, compiled once wasi-sdk is
available); until then the pure-Python v9 carries the depth-N beam and already
fits the 8 s cumulative budget, so WASM here is a proven pipeline + headroom
lever, not a current necessity.

--- v9 (inherited) ---
Training-free potential-field controller. v9 = v8 (gravity-well FEED, virus
weaponization, v7 death-zone layer + reachability virus gate) plus a **shallow
tactical search** that looks ``SEARCH_DEPTH`` frames ahead to avoid forced death
— the "death in N frames" analogue of a chess engine detecting mate-in-N.

Why a search on top of the field: the v8 field + its 1-frame ``_safe_heading``
floor cannot see a move that is safe *this* frame but loses next frame — most
sharply, our own split-to-kill fragmenting us into half-mass blobs that a nearby
predator's split then eats one frame later (``_should_split`` only checked our
centre, not where the kill-child lands, nor the enemy's split reply). A depth>=2
lookahead that MODELS the enemy's split response catches exactly that.

Design (see the module for detail):
* **Policy prior + leaf eval = the v8 field.** ``_policy`` still produces the
  preferred heading/split; the search only reorders/vetoes among candidates and
  scores quiet leaves with the field's own notion of good (mass, alignment).
* **We branch, opponents are modelled.** Our moves discretise into a heading
  fan (``SEARCH_DIRS``) + the policy's intended split; each of the nearest
  ``SEARCH_MAX_THREATS`` eaters gets ONE modelled reply per node (chase, and
  **split toward us when a split-child would land the eat within reach**), so
  the tree is ``beam × dirs × depth`` — no opponent branching blow-up.
* **Adaptive + governed.** Depth scales down with threat count; the search is
  skipped entirely with no threats in view, and a cumulative-time governor
  falls back to the pure v8 reactive move as the 8 s game budget is approached.
* **Defense in depth.** Any search error falls back to the v8 move, and v8's
  1-frame ``_safe_heading`` still runs as a floor on non-split frames.

``SEARCH_DEPTH`` is a module-level constant (mirrored by ``search_depth`` on
HeuristicParams) so the lookahead horizon is a single knob to change later.

Hand-authored (NOT generated by tools/package_heuristic.py). Imports the kit
(helper, lib), the Python stdlib, and the whitelisted ``wasmtime`` (guarded —
imported lazily inside the loader so its absence just disables WASM). No `bot`
package, no numpy, no learned weights.
"""

